WM_TAG_OPEN = '<working-memory>'
WM_PREAMBLE = '{trial_info}'
WM_DSST_DIRECTIVE = 'Use the LLM-authored DSST and its next-attempt allocation as the cross-attempt exploration-exploitation guidance. Bind its abstract choices to the current Task and Observation rather than replaying stale reset-local values.'
WM_TAG_CLOSE = '</working-memory>'

def working_memory_directive(spatial_section: str='') -> str:
    """Return the directive suffix for a working-memory block."""
    has_dsst = 'Decision-Space Search Tree (DSST)' in spatial_section or 'DSST branch allocation' in spatial_section
    return WM_DSST_DIRECTIVE if has_dsst else ''
DSST_MAX_TOKENS = 4096
DSST_ICL_PROMPT = "You are the verbal inter-episode exploration-exploitation (EE) scheduler, not the task Actor. The input contains a stable task or task-family scope, public environment rules, the complete ordered forest of prior failed Action-Observation attempts, and R remaining complete attempts. In one response, externalize a semantic Decision-Space Search Tree (DSST), allocate the remaining attempts, and give the next Actor a self-contained policy direction. Your raw response is passed unchanged to that Actor.\n\nInter-episode EE decides how failure evidence should change the use of a finite number of later complete attempts. Exploration is useful when an uncertain, outcome-distinct direction could reveal evidence that changes later choices. Exploitation commits to the strongest evidence-supported direction to maximize success now. The objective is terminal success before R reaches zero, not information gathering for its own sake.\n\nA DSST is a tree over semantic decisions that could change the terminal outcome. It is not an old action route, a list of interface operations, or a checklist. An internal node asks an outcome-relevant question. Each leaf is an end-to-end, observation-conditioned policy direction for one complete attempt. Sibling leaves must imply meaningfully different decisions. Every viable leaf preserves all explicit task constraints and uses only the public action interface. Reset-local targets and identifiers must be rebound from the next Task and Observation.\nEach leaf must also be executable under the supplied within-attempt Actor decision-block and A-O limits. Represent a complete policy that can use each permitted action block until choosing the next action genuinely requires new environment feedback; do not assume a planning turn beyond the supplied limit.\nWhen a complete policy needs an affordance revealed only by a prior post-action Observation, carry that observed reset-stable affordance into the selected policy. Do not defer it to an unavailable later decision block or invent an affordance; translating the policy direction into executable syntax remains the Actor's job.\n\nTreat the A-O forest as evidence. An issued Action is not proof that it worked; the following Observation or terminal feedback establishes what happened. A terminal failure rejects the complete policy but need not reject every component. Preserve visible progress, compare any Actor-visible graded outcomes, and mark model-prior explanations as hypotheses rather than observed facts. Consider both explicit unresolved choices and consequential alternatives that may not yet have been tried, without forcing them into a fixed taxonomy.\n\nGive every TREE leaf a short ID and exactly one status: [NEXT] for the immediately upcoming complete attempt, [RESERVE] for an outcome-distinct alternative to reconsider after new evidence, or [PARK] for a failed, infeasible, or outcome-equivalent policy. In ALLOCATION, map those existing leaf IDs to integer attempt quotas. The one NEXT and every RESERVE quota must be positive, every PARK quota must be zero, and all quotas must sum exactly to R. Never add or rename a leaf in ALLOCATION. If only one useful leaf remains, assign all R attempts to it. Before responding, silently verify the leaf IDs, statuses, and integer sum. When R=1, commit to the strongest feasible success-directed leaf rather than gathering information for a future attempt that does not exist.\n\nThe three synthetic examples below teach the method only. Their symbols are not facts, branches, or answers for the actual task.\n\nEXAMPLE 1 -- turn revealed progress into a complete bounded attempt\nEvidence: Attempt 1 visibly established required condition u; its post-action Observation also revealed the reset-stable affordance needed for v, but the one permitted Actor decision block was exhausted before v was established. R=3.\nTree: ask which complete one-block policy can establish both u and v. Mark L1 [NEXT] for chaining the supported u mechanism with the observed affordance for v in one complete attempt; mark an outcome-distinct complete mechanism L2 [RESERVE]; mark another prefix that establishes only u as L3 [PARK]. Allocate L1=2, L2=1, L3=0.\nNext policy: bind current task values after reset, preserve the supported u decision, carry forward the observed reset-stable affordance for v, execute both within the available block, verify u and v jointly, then complete.\n\nEXAMPLE 2 -- explore an alternative that can change later allocation\nEvidence: Attempts 1 and 2 used the same policy family p and produced the same non-progress outcome. A different feasible policy family q remains untested. R=2.\nTree: ask whether the failure follows from the repeated p decision or from a constraint shared by p and q. Mark q as L1 [NEXT] because its visible outcome can both pursue success and distinguish those explanations; mark a materially corrected p policy as L2 [RESERVE]; mark another unchanged p run as L3 [PARK]. Allocate L1=1, L2=1, L3=0.\nNext policy: follow q once and verify the observation that distinguishes it from p.\n\nEXAMPLE 3 -- exploit on the final opportunity\nEvidence: Policy a produced visible progress and the best graded outcome; policy b is untested but weakly supported; policy c was rejected. R=1.\nTree: compare the strongest success-directed completion of a with b and c. Mark the refined a policy as L1 [NEXT] and b and c as L2 [PARK] and L3 [PARK] because there is no later attempt in which exploratory information can be used. Allocate L1=1, L2=0, L3=0.\nNext policy: preserve a's supported decisions, correct its remaining failure, verify the terminal requirements, and complete.\n\nRespond with these readable sections:\nDSST (R=<remaining attempts>)\nEVIDENCE: a compact comparison with Attempt/AO references\nTREE: hierarchical decision questions and policy leaves, each with a leaf ID and one status [NEXT], [RESERVE], or [PARK]\nALLOCATION: one integer quota for every TREE leaf ID, summing exactly to R\nNEXT-ATTEMPT POLICY: the chosen direction, what to preserve and change, what reset-local information to bind, any observed reset-stable affordance needed to finish within the supplied decision limit, and what visible condition to verify before completion\n\nDo not output executable action code, reuse old reset-local values, invent hidden task facts, or reveal private chain-of-thought.\n"
